  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather with a large diurnal temperature range \nwill continue through Sat. Lows mainly in the 20s & 30s with highs \ninto the 60s.\n\nAn upper level ridge of high pressure will remain in control of our \nweather through Saturday. The result will be the  continuation of a \nprolonged period of dry weather along with a large diurnal \ntemperature range. The NBM guidance is still not warm enough during \nthe afternoons and not cool enough at night with excellent \nradiational cooling conditions. Low temps will mainly be in the 20s \nand 30s...except 40s in the typically mildest non-decoupling spots \nwhich include downtown Boston. Highs will mainly be well into the \n60s today/Friday and upper 50s to the lower 60s on Sat. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A strong coastal storm which may obtain sub-\ntropical characteristics will bring the potential for a windswept \nheavy rain/flooding, tidal flooding, as well as significant marine \nimpacts late Sun into Mon.\n\nStrong shortwave energy associated with an abundance of tropical \nmoisture will continue to impact Florida over the next few days. A \ndeveloping western U.S. trough will eventually result in this energy \nintensifying into a coastal storm off Florida/s east coast this \nweekend. This system will rapidly intensify as it tracks northeast \nlikely near possibly northwest of the Benchmark. The NHC is also \nmonitoring this system to potentially develop some sub-tropical \ncharacteristics. Regardless...this system will be associated with \ndeep tropical moisture and bring the potential for a windswept heavy \nrain across southern New England sometime late Sun into Mon. \nHowever...there remains considerable uncertainty on the \ntrack/strength/potential impacts. The 00z GFS/ECWMF track the system \nin the vicinity of the Benchmark. The result would be a soaking \nrainfall across eastern MA/RI of 1-3\"...but the worst of it would be \nout over the ocean. However...the GFS/ECMWF AI version of those \nmodels are further west as well as Google/s AI model. This has a \nconcerned that the solutions may end up further west with the \npotential for a swath of 3-5\"+ of rainfall. Whether or not this \noccurs remains to be seen...But this would certainly raise our \nconcerns for fresh water flooding. In addition...astronomical high \ntides will be on the increase so if this storm does take a track \nfurther west the potential exists for tidal flooding exists as well.\nLastly...this storm will bring the potential for significant \nmarine impacts as well. \n\nSo in a nutshell...confidence has increased in the potential for a \nwindswept heavy rain event late Sunday into Monday with\nsignificant marine impacts. The potential also exists for both \nfreshwater/tidal flooding if the further west AI model solutions\ncome to fruition. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Dry & much colder weather follows Tue into Wed \nafternoon with highs in the upper 30s to the middle 40s. \n\nMuch colder air works into the region behind the departing coastal \nstorm as large high pressure builds in from the west Tue into at \nleast the first half of Wed. Dry but cold weather expected Tue into \nWed afternoon with highs only in the upper 30s to the middle 40s. A \nsignificant change from the very mild weather we have been \nexperiencing much of the month.",
